  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "One Dive",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"One Dive\"**\n\nThe page is one dive. Scroll is depth: the visitor starts at the sunlit surface and ends on the floor of the Challenger Deep at 10,935 m, with a depth gauge counting the metres. The colour field is the real ocean zones; the site opens light on purpose.\n\nThe world is a bathymetric survey drawn from exact geometry: depth axes, a trench cross-section, plumb lines, a porthole gauge, a sonar scope. One character lives in it: Otto, an octopus in a bathysphere, who is the bot.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "The background darkens with depth.",
      "Buoy orange marks what is live or clickable; kelp marks the hold.",
      "Ink in light water, foam in the deep.",
      "Pills for controls, circles for instruments, 20px hulls.",
      "Scroll-driven motion with still fallbacks."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Depth Rule",
        "body": "A section's background is the zone it sits in; the page only gets darker going down (the kelp hold band is a lit object, not water).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Buoy Rule",
        "body": "Orange means live or actionable, never body copy or decoration. Otto is the one exception.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ink Flip Rule",
        "body": "Text flips from ink to foam only while no text is being read.",
        "section": "colors"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do keep each section in its zone colour.",
      "Do reserve orange for live state and the primary action.",
      "Do set changing numbers in mono with tabular numerals.",
      "Do draw instruments from exact geometry.",
      "Do gate all motion behind prefers-reduced-motion."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't open on a dark crypto hero.",
      "Don't put foam text on shallow or surf water.",
      "Don't use eyebrow labels above headings.",
      "Don't add neon glows, glass or gradient text.",
      "Don't use the legacy alias tokens on public surfaces."
    ]
  }
}